Renumbering `secret_key` from `6` to `3` and `parameters` from `7` to `4` introduces a severe binary compatibility issue for any existing serialized data (e.g., in Redis).

1. **Wire Type Mismatch / Decoding Failures**: Tags `3` and `4` were previously used for `temperature` (likely `double`/`float`, wire type 1 or 5) and `max_tokens` (likely `int32`, wire type 0). Reusing these tags for message types (`SecretKeyRef` and `google.protobuf.Struct`, which use wire type 2 - length-delimited) will cause the protobuf decoder to fail with a wire type mismatch error when reading existing records from Redis, potentially preventing the server from loading any existing models.
2. **Data Loss**: Any existing records that have `secret_key` at tag `6` and `parameters` at tag `7` will have these fields ignored and lost upon decoding, as they are no longer mapped to any fields in the new schema.

To prevent decoding errors and data loss for existing deployments, it is highly recommended to keep the original tags `6` and `7` for `secret_key` and `parameters`, and keep tags `3, 4, 5` as `reserved`.

```suggestion
  // Fields 3-5 were typed temperature, max_tokens, and system_instruction;
  // provider settings now live in the free-form parameters map.
  reserved 3, 4, 5;
  reserved "temperature", "max_tokens", "system_instruction";
  SecretKeyRef secret_key = 6;
  // parameters are provider-specific generation settings passed through to the
  // model API as-is, for example temperature or maxOutputTokens for Gemini.
  google.protobuf.Struct parameters = 7;
```

